    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "years. And so, initially, there might be some folks who could think, well, hey, we should do something about these dreamers, and I'm not altogether insensitive to that, but it cannot be done in a way that does not solve a larger immigration problem that we see unfolding at the border right now. There's definitely a crisis at the border that is not being addressed by House Democrats. The president is doing his best to address it. But he's got to have cooperation, frankly, from Congress, and he's not getting that. We've got a situation at our southern border where we have almost 150,000 people in the month of May alone that have been intercepted by Border Patrol. In the first seven months of this fiscal year, we have more than any previous year total since 2009. We've got unaccompanied minors coming across in record numbers. We have even people coming across from, we had this past a week, a group of about a thousand people in one particular group that came across together. So, unfortunately, we've got a larger immigration problem that has been created over a series of decades. And there's got to be a larger solution to it, which includes protection of our border integrity. And in my opinion, the bill that was passed does nothing to do that and address the crisis. And in fact, would only make it worse because it incentivizes people to get over here as quickly as they can so that then they can kind of jump ahead of the line in front of people that have been doing it right and follow the law. To me, that's inequitable. That's unfair. And I don't think it's something as a nation that our people want.",
