  {
    "episode_id": 84197,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84197.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think it's a fair question. I think it's got to be answered though in the context of what actually is required to get a bill passed through the House and the Senate. Many people may not know this, but the Senate right now has something called culture, right? where you can't even get a bill heard or at least voted on until you have at least 60 members of the Senate, which will close the debate. That's called cloture. So, yes, did we have majority in the Senate the last two years before this last election? Yes, but we did not have that 60 votes necessary to close the debate and actually have a vote. And so we had the majority, the simple majority, but unfortunately the existing rules of the Senate, we can have a simple majority of the Senate and the House and still not be able to get anything done because you've got at least 41 members of the Senate who refuse to allow a vote on an issue. So many people don't understand that. But that in fact, I think is what has probably been the issue that's made it very difficult to solve this problem. And so now that's not an excuse. But I think to simply say that you had the majority, the simple majority and couldn't get it done doesn't really accurately kind of characterize what exactly, you know, the rules are in the",
