  {
    "episode_id": 84197,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84197.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Right, absolutely. So this is a critical industry for us. It's a critical issue for me. And so their ability to have access to labor is important. So yes, do we need people to come in to do that? Absolutely. But right now, the process is broken from a number of different respects. We can't really control what the people that are coming, about one out of every 10, it's told to me once they get into the country to provide, after they've given those work visas, They take off. And so maybe we need to have a situation where we incentivize them to stay by holding some of the pay, right? Or give them an incentive at the end to where if they go back to their nation after the temporary visa, then they're given some incentive. So some of these types of things where we're allowing our ag industry to have access to the labor needed but also not providing them frankly and incentive to stay over. So, but that's an important issue. I think that has to be addressed in any larger immigration package, as well as the integrity of the border, as well as to make the application process quicker, more streamlined so that people can know exactly what's necessary and have some incredible understanding and appreciation for how long it's going to take. And then maybe tweaking of what the criteria are for people to be admitted into the country. I might add that one of the things included in HR6, and I think I mentioned it earlier, but I'll say it again, it allows people in this TPS temporary status to become permanent residents. That is never what TPS was intended to do. It was only intended to make sure that people could come here temporarily if they had a major war in actual catastrophe in their own homeland. I think that would be an undue broadening of what the intention of that legislation was initially. And again, incentivizing people to come here illegally. That is the last thing we need right now, particularly with what's going on at the crisis of our southern border.",
